> I couldn't find this in the documentation. What exactly does this parameter do? When should I use it? Is it Bybit-specific and/or can I safely use that with other exchanges as well? (I'm developing an application that works with many different exchanges, and I'm trying to keep the code as generic as possible, hence the question.)

@hodlerhacks Some exchanges have different endpoints to handle stop/trigger orders, so the `stop` parameter tells ccxt to use that endpoint instead, and yes it is unified across exchanges so you should be able to use it with other exchanges as well.

Additionally, we will try to improve our documentation, thanks for letting us know.

Hello @danieltorscho,
Bybit is one of the only exchanges loading derivative features (markets, balances, etc) by default. I suspect that your funds are under the spot wallet, so I'd suggest instantiating bybit like this:

```Python
exchange = ccxt.bybit({
    'apiKey': 'YOUR_API_KEY',
    'secret': 'YOUR_SECRET_KEY',
    'options': {
       'defaultType': 'spot', # very important set spot as default type
    }
})
balances = await exchange.fetch_balance()
```
